=== SHAPES PER OP (representative + golden-tractable; GPU runs them fast, NumPy golden is the cost) ===
rmsnorm:                (256,512)+w(512)   | (130,127) odd last dim -> scalar tail | (2,64,128)+w(128) 3-D flattens
gelu:                   (64,128)           | (3,127) odd element count -> scalar tail
matmul:                 (128,128)@(128,128) square | (256,128)@(128,256) rectangular | (130,65)@(65,127) non-tile-multiple (bounds checks)
                        | (2,64,128)@(128,64) batched flattens | (256,256)@(256,512) large-K reduction-order class (calibrated ceiling, see below)
softmax:                (64,256) tokens-vocab | (32,127) odd last dim | (2,16,64) 3-D flattens
fused_rmsnorm_matmul:   (128,128)@(128,128) square | (130,65)@(65,127) non-tile (rmsnorm over odd K=65)
fused_matmul_bias_gelu: (128,128)@(128,128)+bias(128) square | (130,65)@(65,127)+bias(127) non-tile (per-column bias on odd N=127)

max_rel_err CALIBRATION (mirrors committed test_cpu_ref_fused.py): the single-op
gate max_rel_err <= 1e-2 assumes ONE reduction summed in NumPy's order. The tiled
GEMM accumulates over K in BLOCK_K tiles (a different fp32 summation order), so at
large K an ISOLATED element can land 1 bf16 ULP off at small magnitude — with
cosine == pcc == 1.0 and >= 99.99% bit-exact (NOT a bug; a real bug tanks
cosine/pcc toward 0). test_hip_matmul_large_k_reduction_order documents this class
with the same calibrated ceiling (5e-2) the committed CPU fused test uses; every
other matmul shape (K <= 128) passes the FULL assert_correct.

